From 6e23d97a0b42493bec9658849b0d3aff151f1877 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aaron Bell <18724+air@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 14:48:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Make a state load carry on exactly where the save left off.

A load restored the 6502, memory and the chip registers, but not
where the machine was in time. The tick count restarted from zero,
the VIA, video and ADC timers and the 6845 counters were rescheduled
from scratch, and the ACIA and serial ULA stayed at power-on. So a
loaded machine soon ran differently from the one that saved it.

Most of this has no field in the B-em format, so it goes in a block
in the second half of the 64k RAM field. beebjit used to leave that
memory zero, and it loads only the first 32k of the field; B-em and
jsbeeb load it into RAM that a model B never maps. So the file stays
a B-em 2.x snapshot. The block carries a signature, its size and a
CRC over the B-em fields it refines. If any of them don't match, the
load uses the B-em fields alone, as before.

The block holds the tick and cycle counts, the VIA timers, ports and
control lines, the 6845 counters and flags, any ADC conversion in
progress, the SN76489 bus state, the ACIA and serial ULA state, and
whether the CPU had already committed to an interrupt at the
instruction boundary of the save.

For other readers, the save also fills the B-em fields it can: IC32
(which held the system VIA's port B inputs), the VIA input registers
and CA1 / CA2, and the 6845 counters. The load sets IC32 from its
field. Setting the 6845 registers on load also updates the values
derived from them, as a register write does.

Disc and tape drive state isn't carried.

Adds a functional test: the RVI rendering check across a save and a
load, in interp and JIT modes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
